# Resumate — Cursor project rules

## TypeScript

- Enforce strict TypeScript types. Do not use `any`. Prefer explicit interfaces, discriminated unions, and `unknown` with narrowing when the type is genuinely unknown.
- Prefer `satisfies` and Zod-inferred types (`z.infer<typeof Schema>`) over hand-rolled loose types.

## Groq / AI outputs

- Mandate Zod schema validation on all Groq API JSON outputs before passing them to the frontend or the LaTeX compiler.
- Never trust model JSON as-is. Parse with Zod; on failure, return a clear error to the caller and do not forward partial or unvalidated data.
- Handle Groq rate limits gracefully: catch HTTP 429 / quota errors, surface a user-safe message, and avoid retry storms.

## LaTeX compilation

- Handle LaTeX compilation errors gracefully. Never crash the request path on pdflatex failure.
- Return structured errors (message, optional line/hint) suitable for the editor UI.
- Keep compile work ephemeral; do not persist user PDFs or sources as shared server state.

## Resume state vs rendering

- Separate raw JSON resume state from LaTeX template rendering string logic.
- Treat structured resume data (JSON / typed objects) as the source of truth for content when that path exists.
- Keep LaTeX template rendering as a pure transform from structured state (or validated AI patches) into a TeX string — do not mix unstructured string mutation with schema-backed state updates in the same layer.
